        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the requirements again. The requirement states:\n\n> \"prefer a repository digest if available, otherwise a repository tag, and fall back to the raw image ID only when no other name exists.\"\n\nBut then it also says:\n\n> \"If an image already exists in the metadata store, its name should be updated only when the newly selected preferred name is more readable (i.e., does not contain a raw sha256 string).\"\n\nThere's a tension here. A repo digest contains \"sha256:\", so according to the readability rule, it's NOT more readable than a repo tag. Let me re-read the requirements more carefully.\n\nActually, I think I misunderstood. The priority for SELECTING the preferred name is: digest > tag > ID. But the rule for UPDATING an existing name is different - only update if the new name is more readable (no sha256).\n\nSo the logic should be:\n1. When first creating an image event, use the preferred name (digest > tag > ID)\n2. When an image already exists, only update the name if the new preferred name is more readable than the existing one\n\nBut wait, if we prefer digests, and digests contain sha256, then we'd never update from a digest to a tag. Let me think about this more carefully...\n\nActually, re-reading: \"does not contain a raw sha256 string\" - I think \"raw sha256 string\" means a pure image ID like `sha256:abc123...`, not a repo digest like `repo@sha256:abc123...`. A repo digest is more user-friendly than a raw image ID because it includes the repository name.\n\nLet me refine the `isNameMoreReadable` function to better capture this:\n\n",
